===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*trewą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*drew-o-}}, a suffixed variant of {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*drew-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*deru-||solid, steadfast}}, whence probably also {{etyl|ine-pro|-}} noun for "tree" {{m|ine-pro|*dóru}}. Germanic cognates include {{cog|ofs|trē}}, {{cog|osx|treo}}, {{cog|non|tré}} ({{cog|da|træ}}), {{cog|got|𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{cog|grc|δόρυ||wood, spear}}, {{m|grc|δρῦς||tree, oak}}, {{cog|cu|дрѣво|sc=Cyrs}} ({{cog|ru|де́рево}}), {{cog|sga|daur}} ({{cog|cy|derwen||oak}}).

